# AST matcher for the resident-module teardown bug.
#
# An ndl .luax module stays loaded after its main returns, but crt0.S runs __cpp_fini and newlib
# exit as soon as main returns. That destroys every static object in the image while the image is
# still registered and callable, so the module's globals (and, in luagiac, all of giac's) are freed
# under later calls. luagiac.c had this and reset the calculator on the first caseval. nucleus.h
# lines 127 to 129 state the rule: a resident program must end main with _exit, not return.
#
# A main that reaches nl_lua_getstate is a Lua module entry point. Two matchers judge it, because
# either alone has a hole the other closes.
#
# The first flags a main with no _exit or _Exit anywhere, which is the whole-function omission.
match functionDecl(
  isMain(),
  isExpansionInMainFile(),
  hasDescendant(callExpr(callee(functionDecl(hasName("nl_lua_getstate"))))),
  unless(hasDescendant(callExpr(callee(functionDecl(hasAnyName("_exit", "_Exit"))))))
)
# The second flags a return that is a direct statement of main's body. One _exit on a refusal branch
# used to satisfy the first matcher for the whole function, which is how lua_module.cc left its own
# coverage the moment its integrity refusal gained an _exit. A conditional early return before the
# module is registered is correct and stays nested, so only the unconditional fall-out is flagged.
match returnStmt(
  hasParent(compoundStmt(hasParent(functionDecl(
    isMain(),
    isExpansionInMainFile(),
    hasDescendant(callExpr(callee(functionDecl(hasName("nl_lua_getstate")))))))))
)
